From 2e27571902aa04ab055d64c5fe7f2725b4a32834 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Filyus <filyus@filyus.ru>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 14:46:14 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add opts.getAuthorization, a callback that supplies the
 Authorization header

A caller can pass an async function that is given `{ regKey, uri }` (an object,
so that fields can be added later) and returns the value of the Authorization
header, or nothing. It is asked for the keys a stored credential for the request
would be looked up under, longest first: the keys of the request's URL, then,
for a URL on the registry's own host (a tarball), the keys of the registry. The
first value returned is sent. The walk ends at the first key that holds a stored
credential which sets Authorization (a token, _auth, or a username and
password), so the function wins over a stored credential for the same key and a
credential stored under a longer key wins over the function's for a shorter
one. A key that holds only a client certificate and key sets no Authorization,
so an mTLS pair at a longer key does not shadow the function at a shorter one.

The value is sent as a stored credential is: same transport options, same
http: behaviour and same redirects, and the otp retry asks again. It is not
asked for an offline request or when forceAuth is given. An error thrown by the
function rejects the request unchanged. A body that is a promise or a stream
and fails while the function runs rejects the request as well, and a stream is
piped into gzip only once the function is done.

getAuth.supportsGetAuthorization is true, so a caller that cannot do without
the option can tell a library that ignores it.

The library runs no program: a caller that wants a credential from one, such as
npm with a configured tokenHelper, does that in the function.
